{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"53259a99cb9019c2","type":"text","text":"## What is Celery?\n\nThink of Celery as a **task queue system**. Imagine you have a restaurant:\n\n- The **chef** (your main application) takes orders\n- But some dishes take a long time to prepare (like your metadata inference)\n- So the chef writes these orders on tickets and puts them in a **queue**\n- **Kitchen assistants** (Celery workers) pick up these tickets and work on them\n- The chef can continue taking new orders without waiting\n\nIn technical terms:\n\n- **Producer**: Your main app that creates tasks\n- **Broker**: The message queue (like Redis or RabbitMQ) that holds tasks\n- **Worker**: Background processes that execute tasks\n- **Result Backend**: Optional storage for task results\n\n## Key Concepts with Simple Examples\n\n### 1. Tasks\n\nA task is just a function that runs in the background:\n\n```python\n# Instead of running this immediately:\ndef process_data():\n    # Heavy computation that takes 5 minutes\n    return result\n\n# Celery lets you do:\n@celery.task\ndef process_data():\n    # Same heavy computation\n    return result\n\n# And call it like:\nresult = process_data.delay()  # Runs in background!\n```\n\n### 2. Periodic Tasks (Perfect for your CDC!)\n\nThis is exactly what you need - tasks that run automatically on a schedule:\n\n```python\n# Your CDC check every 10 minutes\n@celery.task\ndef check_for_new_data():\n    # Connect to MongoDB\n    # Check for new documents since last check\n    # If found, trigger metadata inference\n    pass\n\n# Schedule it to run every 10 minutes\n```\n\n### 3. Workers\n\nThese are separate processes that actually do the work:\n\n```bash\n# Terminal 1: Your main app runs here\npython app.py\n\n# Terminal 2: Celery worker runs here\ncelery -A your_app worker --loglevel=info\n\n# Terminal 3: Celery scheduler runs here (for periodic tasks)\ncelery -A your_app beat --loglevel=info\n```\n\n## Architecture for Your CDC Use Case\n\n```\n┌─────────────────┐    ┌─────────────┐    ┌──────────────────┐\n│   Your App      │    │   Broker    │    │  Celery Worker   │\n│                 │    │  (Redis)    │    │                  │\n│ Schedules CDC   │───▶│   Queue     │───▶│ Checks MongoDB   │\n│ every 10 min    │    │             │    │ Runs inference   │\n└─────────────────┘    └─────────────┘    └──────────────────┘\n```\n\n## Simple Real-World Example\n\nLet's say you run an e-commerce site and want to update product recommendations every hour:\n\n```python\nfrom celery import Celery\nfrom datetime import datetime\n\n# Create Celery app\napp = Celery('ecommerce')\n\n@app.task\ndef update_recommendations():\n    print(f\"Updating recommendations at {datetime.now()}\")\n    # Your heavy ML computation here\n    return \"Recommendations updated!\"\n\n# This would be in your config\nfrom celery.schedules import crontab\napp.conf.beat_schedule = {\n    'update-recommendations': {\n        'task': 'update_recommendations',\n        'schedule': crontab(minute=0),  # Every hour\n    },\n}\n```\n\n## Why Celery for Your CDC?\n\n1. **Non-blocking**: Your main app doesn't freeze during metadata inference\n2. **Reliable**: If worker crashes, tasks can be retried\n3. **Scalable**: Add more workers to handle increased load\n4. **Scheduling**: Built-in support for periodic tasks (your 10-minute checks)\n5. **Monitoring**: You can see what tasks are running, failed, etc.\n\n## Your CDC Flow Would Look Like:\n\n1. **Celery Beat** (scheduler) triggers your CDC task every 10 minutes\n2. **CDC Task** checks MongoDB for new documents since last run\n3. If new data found → **Queue metadata inference task**\n4. **Worker** processes the inference in background\n5. Results stored back to your system\n6. Repeat every 10 minutes\n\n\n","x":-9880,"y":-6220,"width":1380,"height":700},
		{"id":"5d020a88eca5b335","type":"text","text":"```mermaid\ngraph TB\n    subgraph \"Source Systems\"\n        MongoDB[(MongoDB CRM)]\n        OtherDB[(Other DBs)]\n    end\n    \n    subgraph \"CDC Capture Layer\"\n        CDCAgent[CDC Agent/Listener]\n        CDCLog[(CDC_log Collection)]\n    end\n    \n    subgraph \"Event Processing Queues\"\n        PriorityQ[Priority Queue<br/>High Impact Events]\n        StandardQ[Standard Queue<br/>Medium Impact Events]\n        BatchQ[Batch Queue<br/>Low Impact Events]\n        DLQ[Dead Letter Queue<br/>Failed Events]\n    end\n    \n    subgraph \"Processing Engine\"\n        EventRouter{Event Router<br/>& Classifier}\n        \n        subgraph \"Real-time Processors\"\n            TableProcessor[Table Metadata<br/>Processor]\n            ColumnProcessor[Column Metadata<br/>Processor]\n            RelationshipProcessor[Relationship<br/>Inference Processor]\n        end\n        \n        subgraph \"Batch Processors\"\n            EmbeddingProcessor[Embedding<br/>Generator]\n            ReconciliationProcessor[Daily<br/>Reconciliation]\n            CleanupProcessor[Log Cleanup<br/>Processor]\n        end\n    end\n    \n    subgraph \"Metadata Storage\"\n        TableCollection[(Table Collection)]\n        ColumnCollection[(Column Collection)]\n        RelationshipCollection[(Relationship Collection)]\n        EmbeddingStore[(Vector Store)]\n    end\n    \n    subgraph \"Monitoring & Control\"\n        Monitor[Event Monitor<br/>& Dashboard]\n        AlertSystem[Alert System]\n        RetryManager[Retry Manager]\n    end\n    \n    %% Data Flow\n    MongoDB --> CDCAgent\n    OtherDB --> CDCAgent\n    CDCAgent --> CDCLog\n    \n    CDCLog --> EventRouter\n    \n    EventRouter -->|Schema Changes<br/>PK/FK Changes| PriorityQ\n    EventRouter -->|Data Type Changes<br/>New Columns| StandardQ\n    EventRouter -->|Sample Values<br/>Descriptions| BatchQ\n    \n    PriorityQ --> TableProcessor\n    PriorityQ --> ColumnProcessor\n    StandardQ --> ColumnProcessor\n    StandardQ --> RelationshipProcessor\n    BatchQ --> EmbeddingProcessor\n    \n    %% Processing Flow\n    TableProcessor --> ColumnProcessor\n    ColumnProcessor --> RelationshipProcessor\n    RelationshipProcessor --> EmbeddingProcessor\n    \n    %% Storage Updates\n    TableProcessor --> TableCollection\n    ColumnProcessor --> ColumnCollection\n    RelationshipProcessor --> RelationshipCollection\n    EmbeddingProcessor --> EmbeddingStore\n    \n    %% Error Handling\n    TableProcessor -->|Failed| DLQ\n    ColumnProcessor -->|Failed| DLQ\n    RelationshipProcessor -->|Failed| DLQ\n    EmbeddingProcessor -->|Failed| DLQ\n    \n    DLQ --> RetryManager\n    RetryManager --> EventRouter\n    \n    %% Batch Processing\n    ReconciliationProcessor --> TableCollection\n    ReconciliationProcessor --> ColumnCollection\n    ReconciliationProcessor --> RelationshipCollection\n    CleanupProcessor --> CDCLog\n    \n    %% Monitoring\n    EventRouter --> Monitor\n    TableProcessor --> Monitor\n    ColumnProcessor --> Monitor\n    RelationshipProcessor --> Monitor\n    EmbeddingProcessor --> Monitor\n    \n    Monitor --> AlertSystem\n    \n    %% Styling\n    classDef storage fill:#e1f5fe\n    classDef processor fill:#f3e5f5\n    classDef queue fill:#fff3e0\n    classDef monitor fill:#e8f5e8\n    \n    class MongoDB,OtherDB,CDCLog,TableCollection,ColumnCollection,RelationshipCollection,EmbeddingStore storage\n    class TableProcessor,ColumnProcessor,RelationshipProcessor,EmbeddingProcessor,ReconciliationProcessor,CleanupProcessor processor\n    class PriorityQ,StandardQ,BatchQ,DLQ queue\n    class Monitor,AlertSystem,RetryManager monitor\n```\n\n","x":-2640,"y":-7360,"width":2560,"height":1960},
		{"id":"3adc055b2a04b8f4","type":"text","text":"# Prompt\n\nHey!! I have made run_mongodb_metadata_task, run_relationship_inference_task, edge_metadata_task script function scripts. These functions are designed for only one time, now i need to add the Change Data Capture, means lets say my input is mongodb for the first time metadata inference i have the I ll run all the above functions i ll get a metadata inference, now if there is any change like insert, update or delete in either columns or tables how do i process them, how do i trigger them events, lets discuss from starting\n\n1. what method do we use either it is Real-time/Near Real-time Processing(runs the script if the new data triggers) or Batch Processing(runs the script for every 24 hrs). What is the best approach and why?\n\n2. Lets say the i am getting the new data(CDC data) in separate Collection called (CDC_log) Now what all the keys required to make updates in existing table collection/ column collection\n\nas of now i have {\n\n\"table_id\": 1,\n\n\"column_id\": 300,\n\n\"event_type\": \"column_change\",\n\n\"change_type\": \"insert\",\n\n\"last_modified\": \"2025-07-24T14:09:24.595845\",\n\n\"updated_by\": \"cdc\",\n\n\"event_status\": \"N\"\n\n}\n\nNow check with these and tell me what all things required for changing for metadata-inference, for you sake am sharing the sample table and column data\n\nTable data:\n\n{\n\n\"data\": {\n\n\"id\": \"t1\",\n\n\"icon\": \"\",\n\n\"label\": \"Packages\",\n\n\"type\": \"table\",\n\n\"parent_type\": \"source\",\n\n\"parent_id\": \"s1\",\n\n\"parent_name\": \"CRM\",\n\n\"count\": 18,\n\n\"description\": \"Stores information about packages offered to customers.\"\n\n},\n\n\"embedding_input\": \"The Packages table is a critical component of the CRM system, designed to manage and track various packages available for purchase. It includes essential details such as order identifiers, campaign associations, and appeal links, facilitating effective fundraising and promotional analysis. The table is structured with a primary key of order_id, and it contains foreign keys that connect to other tables, enabling a comprehensive view of package-related data. This table supports key business processes by linking packages to specific appeals and promotional campaigns, thus enhancing customer engagement and tracking sales performance.\",\n\n\"tags\": [\n\n\"packages\",\n\n\"CRM\",\n\n\"fundraising\"\n\n],\n\n\"primary_keys\": [\n\n\"order_id\"\n\n],\n\n\"foreign_keys_referencing_tables\": [\n\n\"appeal_id\",\n\n\"campaign_id\",\n\n\"source_id\",\n\n\"constituent_id\"\n\n],\n\n\"primary_keys_referencing_tables\": [\n\n{\n\n\"to_table\": \"Appeal Source Structure\",\n\n\"linking_column\": \"appeal_id\",\n\n\"relation_description\": \"Referenced by Appeal Source Structure: Associates packages with specific appeals to analyze sales performance.\",\n\n\"relationship\": \"many-to-one\"\n\n},\n\n{\n\n\"to_table\": \"Production Season Types\",\n\n\"linking_column\": \"package_season\",\n\n\"relation_description\": \"Referenced by Production Season Types: Links packages to specific production seasons, indicating the availability of packages during those seasons.\",\n\n\"relationship\": \"many-to-one\"\n\n},\n\n{\n\n\"to_table\": \"Promo Quarterly\",\n\n\"linking_column\": \"appeal_id\",\n\n\"relation_description\": \"Referenced by Promo Quarterly: The Promo Quarterly table captures promotional data that may relate to specific packages through their appeals.\",\n\n\"relationship\": \"many-to-one\"\n\n}\n\n],\n\n\"created_by\": \"demo_user\",\n\n\"created_at\": \"2023-10-01T12:00:00Z\",\n\n\"last_updated_by\": \"user_ai\",\n\n\"updated_at\": \"2025-07-28T13:01:04.308614Z\",\n\n\"human_feedback\": [\n\n{\n\n\"user\": \"\",\n\n\"comment\": \"\",\n\n\"timestamp\": \"\"\n\n}\n\n],\n\n\"embedding_vector\": [\n\n-0.043620627373456955,\n\n-0.003634409746155143,\n\n-0.02531168796122074\n\n]\n\n}\n\nColumn Data:\n\n{\n\n\"data\": {\n\n\"id\": \"c1\",\n\n\"label\": \"customer_no\",\n\n\"type\": \"column\",\n\n\"data_type\": \"Integer\",\n\n\"nullable\": false,\n\n\"parent_type\": \"table\",\n\n\"parent_id\": \"t8\",\n\n\"parent_name\": \"Deceased Cont w Last Activity\",\n\n\"description\": \"Unique identifier for each customer.\"\n\n},\n\n\"embedding_input\": \"The 'customer_no' column is an integer type and serves as the primary key for the table, ensuring that each record is uniquely identifiable. This column is critical for linking customer data across various systems and reports. It is non-nullable, indicating that every record must have a valid customer number. This identifier is essential for maintaining data integrity and is often used in relational queries to join with other customer-related tables.\",\n\n\"sample_values\": [\n\n300282,\n\n300616,\n\n300705\n\n],\n\n\"tags\": [\n\n\"customer_id\",\n\n\"primary_key\",\n\n\"identifier\"\n\n],\n\n\"created_by\": \"demo_user\",\n\n\"created_at\": \"2023-10-01T12:00:00Z\",\n\n\"last_updated_by\": \"user_ai\",\n\n\"updated_at\": \"2025-07-28T11:30:10.837316Z\",\n\n\"human_feedback\": [\n\n{\n\n\"user\": \"\",\n\n\"comment\": \"\",\n\n\"timestamp\": \"\"\n\n}\n\n],\n\n\"embedding_vector\": [\n\n0.025610504671931267,\n\n-0.009771500714123249,\n\n-0.009715058840811253\n\n]\n\n}\n\nDO NOT PROVIDE ME THE CODE LETS FIRST DISCUSS THE APPROACH FOR DESIGNING THEN WE CAN GO FOR THE IMPLEMENTATION.","x":-3840,"y":-6800,"width":880,"height":520},
		{"id":"297e0dd79ccd3b0f","type":"text","text":"# CDC\nID's: source_id, column_id, table_id\nUpdated_by: OT / CDC\nevent_status: N/Y\nevent_type: source_change / table_change / column_change\nchange_type: insert / delete \n\n----\n### OT \n*New Tables\nNew Columns*\n### CDC\n*New Table*:\n\tInsert Table\n\tInsert Columns\n*Update Table*:\n\tInsert Column\n\tUpdate Column\n\tDelete Column\n*Delete Table*:\n\tDelete Table\n\n\n\n\n","x":-4140,"y":-4960,"width":860,"height":720},
		{"id":"8184ef3a0eadac96","type":"text","text":"# Step 1: List of All Metadata Change Scenarios\n\n### Stage 1: New Table\n* `Insert Table`\n\n### Stage 2: Update Table\n* `Insert Column`\n* `Update Column`\n* `Delete Column`\n* `Update Table`(e.g., label, description, primary/foreign keys)\n\n### Stage 3: Delete Table\n* `Delete Table`\n\t* Should also trigger deletion of all its columns (or flagging as deleted)\n\n\n# Step 2: Required Fields in CDC Log JSON\n\nFor `event_type: \"new_table\"`\n\n```json\n{\n\"cdc_id\": 1,\n\"table_id\": 1,\n\"column_id\": 23,\n\"event_type\": \"new_table/update_table/delete_table\",\n\"change_type\": \"insert_table/insert_column/update_column/update_table/delete_table\",\n\"last_modified\": \"xx-xx-xx\",\n\"updated_by\": \"AI/ML\",\n\"event_status\": \"Y\"\n}\n```\n\n```json\n{\n\"cdc_id\": 1,\n\"table_id\": 1,\n\"event_type\": \"new_table\",\n\"change_type\": \"insert_column\",\n\"last_modified\": \"xx-xx-xx\",\n\"updated_by\": \"AI/ML\",\n\"event_status\": \"Y\"\n}\n```\n\nFor `event_type: \"column_change\"`\n\n```json\n{\n\"cdc_id\": 1,\n\"table_id\": 1,\n\"column_id\":110,\n\"event_type\": \"table_change\",\n\"change_type\": \"insert\",\n\"last_modified\": \"xx-xx-xx\",\n\"updated_by\": \"AI/ML\",\n\"event_status\": \"Y\"\n}\n```\n\n","x":-2220,"y":-4920,"width":1060,"height":1399},
		{"id":"d272a3e98ef5db68","type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I understand your CDC setup and metadata inference pipeline. Let me break down the approach for implementing this with Celery.\n\n## Understanding Your Data Flow\n\nFrom your CDC log, I can see you're tracking:\n\n- **What changed**: `event_type` (new/update/delete table)\n- **How it changed**: `change_type` (specific operations)\n- **Processing status**: `event_status` (\"N\" = Not processed)\n\n## Celery Architecture Approach\n\n### 1. **Main CDC Periodic Task** (Every 10 minutes)\n\n```\ncheck_cdc_changes() \n├── Query CDC_delta_log for event_status = \"N\"\n├── Group changes by event_type and priority\n└── Dispatch appropriate processing tasks\n```\n\n### 2. **Task Hierarchy Strategy**\n\nInstead of one massive task, break it into a **task chain**:\n\n```\nCDC Detection → Event Processing → Metadata Pipeline → Relationship Inference → Edge Processing\n```\n\n**Why this approach?**\n\n- Each step can be monitored independently\n- If one step fails, you can retry just that part\n- Different steps might need different resources/timing\n\n### 3. **Event-Driven Task Dispatching**\n\nBased on your `event_type`, different processing paths:\n\n```python\n# Conceptual approach\ndef process_cdc_changes():\n    unprocessed = get_unprocessed_cdc_records()\n    \n    for record in unprocessed:\n        if record.event_type == \"new_table\":\n            # Full metadata inference pipeline\n            chain_new_table_processing.delay(record)\n        \n        elif record.event_type == \"update_table\":\n            # Partial inference based on change_type\n            if record.change_type == \"insert_column\":\n                chain_column_processing.delay(record)\n            elif record.change_type == \"update_table\":\n                chain_table_update_processing.delay(record)\n            # ... other update types\n        \n        elif record.event_type == \"delete_table\":\n            # Cleanup and relationship updates\n            chain_delete_processing.delay(record)\n```\n\n### 4. **Task Chains for Different Scenarios**\n\n**For `new_table` (Full Pipeline):**\n\n```\nnew_table_detected \n    → mongodb_metadata_generation \n    → relationship_inference \n    → edge_metadata_inference \n    → mark_cdc_processed\n```\n\n**For `update_table` with `insert_column`:**\n\n```\ncolumn_change_detected \n    → column_metadata_generation \n    → relationship_inference (if needed)\n    → edge_table_to_column_update \n    → mark_cdc_processed\n```\n\n**For `delete_table`:**\n\n```\ndelete_detected \n    → cleanup_relationships \n    → update_edge_metadata \n    → mark_cdc_processed\n```\n\n### 5. **Smart Processing Strategy**\n\nInstead of running everything for every change:\n\n**Incremental Processing:**\n\n- `new_table` → Full pipeline\n- `insert_column` → Only column inference + table-to-column edges\n- `update_column` → Re-infer that column + update relationships\n- `update_table` → Re-infer table metadata + relationships\n- `delete_table` → Cleanup only\n\n### 6. **Error Handling & State Management**\n\n```\nEach task updates CDC record status:\n\"N\" → \"P\" (Processing) → \"C\" (Completed) or \"E\" (Error)\n```\n\n### 7. **Batch Processing Optimization**\n\nInstead of processing one record at a time:\n\n```python\n# Group related changes\ndef group_cdc_changes(records):\n    # If table_id=1 has multiple column updates, batch them\n    # If multiple tables need full inference, process in parallel\n    return grouped_batches\n```\n\n### 8. **Priority-Based Processing**\n\n```\nPriority 1: delete_table (cleanup first)\nPriority 2: new_table (full processing)\nPriority 3: update_table (incremental)\n```\n\n## Key Questions for Implementation:\n\n1. **Concurrency**: Can multiple tables be processed simultaneously, or do you need sequential processing?\n    \n2. **Dependencies**: Does relationship inference need ALL tables to be processed first, or can it work incrementally?\n    \n3. **Rollback**: If metadata inference fails mid-way, do you need to rollback changes?\n    \n4. **Batch Size**: How many CDC records should be processed in one cycle?\n    \n5. **Resource Management**: Are there memory/computation limits for your inference steps?\n    \n\n## Proposed Task Structure:\n\n```\ncelery_app/\n├── tasks/\n│   ├── cdc_monitor.py          # Main 10-min periodic task\n│   ├── metadata_inference.py   # Your 3-step pipeline\n│   ├── relationship_tasks.py   # Relationship inference\n│   └── edge_tasks.py          # Edge metadata processing\n├── utils/\n│   ├── cdc_processor.py       # CDC record handling\n│   └── task_chains.py         # Chain definitions\n└── config.py                  # Celery configuration\n```\n\nDoes this approach align with your thinking? Which part would you like to dive deeper into first - the CDC monitoring logic, the task chaining strategy, or the incremental processing approach?","x":-6600,"y":-6800,"width":1880,"height":1480},
		{"id":"a39154f3d71df1a5","type":"text","text":"# Celery Beat and Celery Worker \n\nA **celery_worker** is a process that constantly listens for tasks (also called jobs) from the message broker (e.g., Redis, RabbitMQ) and executes the specific Python functions (tasks) you define in your application\n\n**What does it do?**\n\n- Pulls tasks from the queue\n    \n- Runs those tasks (executes your code)\n    \n- Can scale horizontally: you can run multiple workers (even across machines), and each can process tasks as they come in\n    \n- Handles task retries, failures, and progress reporting\n\n\n**celery_beat** is a scheduler process that kicks off (schedules) tasks at periodic intervals that you define—similar to a cron job, but managed inside your application\n\n- **What does it do?**\n    \n    - Keeps track of scheduled intervals for your tasks\n        \n    - When it’s time to run a task (e.g., every 10 minutes), celery_beat will send that task to the broker/queue\n        \n    - The actual execution will be picked up by one or more celery_worker processes\n        \n    - You typically only need **one** celery_beat running for your system\n\n\n\n## How They Work Together\n\n1. **celery_beat** schedules periodic tasks and puts them onto the message broker at the correct times.\n    \n2. **celery_worker** listens to the broker, pulls in any waiting tasks, and runs the code.\n    \n\n**Think of celery_beat as the “scheduler” (like a project manager), and celery_worker as the “worker” (the one actually doing the jobs).**","x":-10640,"y":-5080,"width":1380,"height":880},
		{"id":"c0941a0ab6148f12","type":"text","text":"# Note On Running things\n\nIf the event type is either delete or insert append/delete them\n\n\nIf the event type is update run the task. \n\nCDC Batch Size\n\n","x":-6043,"y":-7378,"width":563,"height":358},
		{"id":"2d1e10767761aa42","type":"text","text":"```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    A[Celery Beat triggers check_cdc_changes every 10 min] --> B[check_cdc_changes runs]\n    B --> C[Get unprocessed CDC records]\n    C --> D{Event Type?}\n    D -->|NEW_TABLE| E[Dispatch process_new_table]\n    D -->|UPDATE_TABLE| F[Dispatch process_table_update]\n    D -->|DELETE_TABLE| G[Dispatch process_table_deletion]\n    E & F & G --> H[Update record status]\n    H --> I[Return processing summary]\n```\n","x":-8160,"y":-7900,"width":980,"height":980},
		{"id":"e94581762346e6dd","type":"text","text":"# Delete Table\n\nStep 1: Find the table which you have received as input and delete it.\nStep 2: Find the tables which are related to it and update them.\nStep 3: Find the  columns which are columns of deleting table and delete them.\nStep 4: Update the Embedding vector.\n\n\n\n\nLets say i want to delete the table name called Packages i want to delete the everything that particular table is related.\nfor example if the table packages is related with other table you need to update that table, then you need to delete the columns related to that table","x":-5160,"y":-7500,"width":840,"height":540},
		{"id":"d9061e23075976cb","type":"text","text":"# Prompt 3\nHeyy!!! I need to Design the architecture for change data capture (CDC) i already have the one time metadata inference where in:\n\nSTEP 1: I am inferring the metadata inference in that we are having non inferred data in mongodb and from there we are taking as a input and inferring the description, embedding input, tags, embedding vector, foreign_keys_referencing_tables, last_updated_by, updated_at in table data and in column data we are inferring the column description, embedding_input, embedding_vector, tags, last_updated_by, updated_at and storing them in same db.\n\nSTEP 2: I am inferring the relationship data for every table foreign_keys_referencing_tables, primary_keys_referencing_tables, last_updated_by, updated_at, here in foreign_keys_referencing_tables, lets say it is a object we are adding key, values in that list \"to_table\", \"linking_column\", \"relation_description\", \"relationship\" and primary_keys_referencing_tables we are adding key, values in that list \"target_table_name\", \"linking_column\", \"relation_description\", \"relationship\".\n\nSTEP 3: We are generating the link between the \"source to table\" and \"table to column\"\n\nThese things i have made only for one time, now i need to make it automation, lets say if any one want to update, delete or insert anything, the process should be automatically done. So i am choosing celery for doing that i need you to design the project structure and flow of the execution in my existing code. I will provide you the existing folder structure then in that project structure only you add the celery automation.\n\nNow I have the three \"event_types\": \"new_table\", \"update_table\", \"delete_table\"\n\nI have \"change_type\" for \"new_table\" is \"insert_table\", \"change_type\" for \"update_table\" are \"insert_column\", \"update_column\", \"delete_column\", \"update_column\" and \"change_type\" for \"delete_table\" is \"delete_table\".\n\nThese are am getting in the collection called \"CDC_delta_log\" from mongodb.\n\nBelow is the sample document of single event from CDC_delta_log json {\n\n\"cdc_id\": 1,\n\n\"table_id\": 1,\n\n\"column_id\": 23,\n\n\"event_type\": \"new_table/update_table/delete_table\",\n\n\"change_type\": \"insert_table/insert_column/update_column/update_table/delete_table\",\n\n\"last_modified\": \"xx-xx-xx\",\n\n\"updated_by\": \"AI/ML\",\n\n\"event_status\": \"Y\"\n\n}\n\nNow lets start with the architecture step by step.\n\nNow i will share the folder structure of the current existing project\n\nmain.py,\n\napp.py,\n\nutils/config.py, helper_functions.py, llm_query.py, load_prompt.py, logger.py, parse_response.py,\n\nservices/mongodb_processor.py, run_edge_metadata_task.py, run_relationship_inference_task.py, prompts/clean_table_name.txt, mongo_system_prompt.txt, relationship_system_prompt.txt\n\nPOINTS TO NOTE DOWN:\n\n1. Process the periodic task for every 5 minutes\n\n2. Task chains for different scenarios\n\n3. Batch Processing Optimization\n\n4. Priority based processing\n\nWhat you have to do is \"first provide me the folder structure for automation\", \"Lets start with the priority based for example if the first priority is delete table then we need to see the what are the possibilites we have to ulter in database\" \"also exaplain the flow of that 'event_type'\".","x":-4320,"y":-5640,"width":948,"height":560},
		{"id":"bbc5e70602ef32ac","type":"text","text":"```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    A[CDC_delta_log Collection] --> B[Celery Beat Scheduler]\n    B --> C[monitor_cdc_events.delay]\n    C --> D{Event Type?}\n    \n    D -->|new_table| E[process_new_table_workflow]\n    D -->|update_table| F[process_update_table_workflow]\n    D -->|delete_table| G[process_delete_table_workflow]\n    \n    E --> H[Execute New Table Tasks]\n    F --> I[Execute Update Table Tasks]\n    G --> J[Execute Delete Table Tasks]\n    \n    H --> K[Mark Event Processed]\n    I --> K\n    J --> K\n```\n","x":2960,"y":-5940,"width":1000,"height":900},
		{"id":"7f624e34dfd4d425","type":"text","text":"```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    A[New Table Event] --> B[infer_table_metadata]\n    B --> C[infer_column_metadata for all columns]\n    C --> D[infer_table_relationships]\n    D --> E[generate_source_to_table_links]\n    E --> F[generate_table_to_column_links]\n    F --> G[mark_event_processed]\n    \n    G --> H{Success?}\n    H -->|Yes| I[Event Status: Completed]\n    H -->|No| J[handle_failed_event]\n```\n","x":4286,"y":-5899,"width":554,"height":1079},
		{"id":"ba0c034a8cd6cd22","type":"text","text":"```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    A[Update Table Event] --> B{Change Type?}\n    \n    B -->|insert_column| C[infer_column_metadata]\n    B -->|update_column| D[update_column_metadata]\n    B -->|delete_column| E[cleanup_column_metadata]\n    B -->|update_table| F[update_table_metadata]\n    \n    C --> G[update_relationships]\n    D --> G\n    E --> H[cleanup_relationships]\n    F --> G\n    \n    G --> I[update_links]\n    H --> J[cleanup_links]\n    \n    I --> K[mark_event_processed]\n    J --> K\n```\n","x":5172,"y":-5940,"width":1188,"height":780},
		{"id":"ee924636398d821c","type":"text","text":"# Prompt 2\nOkay, now lets move ahead now i have my separate collection called \"CDC_delta_log\" where am storing the changed data, now i need to write the script for it using celery. I will share you my sample data collection \"CDC_delta_log\" so that you will get a better context.\n\n[{\n\n\"cdc_id\": 1,\n\n\"table_id\": 19,\n\n\"column_id\": null,\n\n\"event_type\": \"new_table\",\n\n\"change_type\": \"insert_table\",\n\n\"last_modified\": \"2025-07-28T11:30:10.837316Z\",\n\n\"updated_by\": \"AI/ML\",\n\n\"event_status\": \"N\"\n\n}, {\n\n\"cdc_id\": 2,\n\n\"table_id\": 2,\n\n\"column_id\": 236,\n\n\"event_type\": \"update_table\",\n\n\"change_type\": \"insert_column\",\n\n\"last_modified\": \"2025-07-28T11:30:10.837316Z\",\n\n\"updated_by\": \"AI/ML\",\n\n\"event_status\": \"N\"\n\n},{\n\n\"cdc_id\": 3,\n\n\"table_id\": 5,\n\n\"column_id\": 23,\n\n\"event_type\": \"update_table\",\n\n\"change_type\": \"update_column\",\n\n\"last_modified\": \"2025-07-28T11:30:10.837316Z\",\n\n\"updated_by\": \"AI/ML\",\n\n\"event_status\": \"N\"\n\n},{\n\n\"cdc_id\": 4,\n\n\"table_id\": 1,\n\n\"column_id\": null,\n\n\"event_type\": \"update_table\",\n\n\"change_type\": \"update_table\",\n\n\"last_modified\": \"2025-07-28T11:30:10.837316Z\",\n\n\"updated_by\": \"AI/ML\",\n\n\"event_status\": \"N\"\n\n},{\n\n\"cdc_id\": 5,\n\n\"table_id\": 1,\n\n\"column_id\": null,\n\n\"event_type\": \"delete_table\",\n\n\"change_type\": \"delete_table\",\n\n\"last_modified\": \"2025-07-28T11:30:10.837316Z\",\n\n\"updated_by\": \"AI/ML\",\n\n\"event_status\": \"N\"\n\n}]\n\nIn total i have three different \"event_type\"\n\n1. new_table\n    \n2. update_table\n    \n3. delete_table\n    \n    Also i have one or more change_type for different event_type\n    \n    1. for new_table: \"insert_table\"\n        \n    2. for update_table: \"insert_column\", \"update_column\", \"delete_column\", \"update_table\"\n        \n    3. for delete_table: \"delete_table\"\n        \n\nNow coming to the metadata inference i have written the python script i ll just pass on a context to you on that:\n\n1. I will first get a non-inferred data in task one i am processing mongodb metadata generation where am inferring the embedding_input, description,embedding_vector,foreign_keys_referencing_tables, and tags for the table data and in column data i am inferring the description, embedding_input, embedding_vector, tags\n    \n2. After the first step am running a relationship inference and am getting the foreign_key_referencing_table and primary_key_referencing_table inference by doing a cosine similarity.\n    \n3. After that i am running the edge metadata inference where first am doing the process_source_to_table_relationships, process_table_to_column_relationships\n    \n\nNow if the new data is added in collection it should go through all the steps above\n\nDO NOT PROVIDE ME WITH DIRECT CODE LETS SEE WITH THE APPROACH.\n","x":-4400,"y":-6200,"width":614,"height":424},
		{"id":"67ca2bb3483c3536","type":"text","text":"# prompt 4\nHey!! I have made run_mongodb_metadata_task, run_relationship_inference_task, edge_metadata_task script function scripts. These functions are designed for only one time,\nSTEP 1: I am inferring the metadata inference in that we are having non inferred data in mongodb and from there we are taking as a input and inferring the description, embedding input, tags, embedding vector, foreign_keys_referencing_tables, last_updated_by, updated_at in table data and in column data we are inferring the column description, embedding_input, embedding_vector, tags, last_updated_by, updated_at and storing them in same db.\n\nSTEP 2: I am inferring the relationship data for every table foreign_keys_referencing_tables, primary_keys_referencing_tables, last_updated_by, updated_at, here in foreign_keys_referencing_tables, lets say it is a object we are adding key, values in that list \"to_table\", \"linking_column\", \"relation_description\", \"relationship\" and primary_keys_referencing_tables we are adding key, values in that list \"target_table_name\", \"linking_column\", \"relation_description\", \"relationship\".\n\nSTEP 3: We are generating the link between the \"source to table\" and \"table to column\"\nTable data:\n{\n\"data\": {\n\"id\": \"t1\",\n\"icon\": \"\",\n\"label\": \"Packages\",\n\"type\": \"table\",\n\"parent_type\": \"source\",\n\"parent_id\": \"s1\",\n\"parent_name\": \"CRM\",\n\"count\": 18,\n\"description\": \"Stores information about packages offered to customers.\"\n},\n\"embedding_input\": \"The Packages table is a critical component of the CRM system, designed to manage and track various packages available for purchase. It includes essential details such as order identifiers, campaign associations, and appeal links, facilitating effective fundraising and promotional analysis. The table is structured with a primary key of order_id, and it contains foreign keys that connect to other tables, enabling a comprehensive view of package-related data. This table supports key business processes by linking packages to specific appeals and promotional campaigns, thus enhancing customer engagement and tracking sales performance.\",\n\"tags\": [\n\"packages\",\n\"CRM\",\n\"fundraising\"\n],\n\"primary_keys\": [\n\"order_id\"\n],\n\"foreign_keys_referencing_tables\": [\n\"appeal_id\",\n\"campaign_id\",\n\"source_id\",\n\"constituent_id\"\n],\n\"primary_keys_referencing_tables\": [\n{\n\"to_table\": \"Appeal Source Structure\",\n\"linking_column\": \"appeal_id\",\n\"relation_description\": \"Referenced by Appeal Source Structure: Associates packages with specific appeals to analyze sales performance.\",\n\"relationship\": \"many-to-one\"\n},\n{\n\"to_table\": \"Production Season Types\",\n\"linking_column\": \"package_season\",\n\"relation_description\": \"Referenced by Production Season Types: Links packages to specific production seasons, indicating the aailability of packages during those seasons.\",\n\"relationship\": \"many-to-one\"\n},\n{\n\"to_table\": \"Promo Quarterly\",\n\"linking_column\": \"appeal_id\",\n\"relation_description\": \"Referenced by Promo Quarterly: The Promo Quarterly table captures promotional data that may relate to specific packages through their appeals.\",\n\"relationship\": \"many-to-one\"\n}\n],\n\"created_by\": \"demo_user\",\n\"created_at\": \"2023-10-01T12:00:00Z\",\n\"last_updated_by\": \"user_ai\",\n\"updated_at\": \"2025-07-28T13:01:04.308614Z\",\n\"human_feedback\": [\n{\n\"user\": \"\",\n\"comment\": \"\",\n\"timestamp\": \"\"\n}\n],\n\"embedding_vector\": [\n-0.043620627373456955,\n-0.003634409746155143,\n-0.02531168796122074\n]\n}\nColumn Data:\n{\n\"data\": {\n\"id\": \"c1\",\n\"label\": \"customer_no\",\n\"type\": \"column\",\n\"data_type\": \"Integer\",\n\"nullable\": false,\n\"parent_type\": \"table\",\n\"parent_id\": \"t8\",\n\"parent_name\": \"Deceased Cont w Last Activity\",\n\"description\": \"Unique identifier for each customer.\"\n},\n\"embedding_input\": \"The 'customer_no' column is an integer type and serves as the primary key for The table, ensuring that each record is uniquely identifiable. This column is critical for linking customer data across various systems and reports. It is non-nullable, indicating that every record must have a valid customer number. This identifier is essential for maintaining data integrity and is often used in relational queries to join with other customer-related tables.\",\n\"sample_values\": [\n300282,\n300616,\n300705\n],\n\"tags\": [\n\"customer_id\",\n\"primary_key\",\n\"identifier\"\n],\n\"created_by\": \"demo_user\",\n\"created_at\": \"2023-10-01T12:00:00Z\",\n\"last_updated_by\": \"user_ai\",\n\"updated_at\": \"2025-07-28T11:30:10.837316Z\",\n\"human_feedback\": [\n{\n\"user\": \"\",\n\"comment\": \"\",\n\"timestamp\": \"\"\n}\n],\n\"embedding_vector\": [\n0.025610504671931267,\n-0.009771500714123249,\n-0.009715058840811253\n]\n}\n\nLet's move ahead now i have my separate collection called \"CDC_delta_log\" where am storing the changed data, now i need to write the script for it using celery. I will share you my sample data collection \"CDC_delta_log\" so that you will get a better context.\n[{\n\"cdc_id\": 1,\n\"table_id\": 19\n\"column_id\": null,\n\"event_type\": \"new_table\"\n\"change_type\": \"insert_table\",\n\"last_modified\": \"2025-07-28T11:30:10.837316Z\"\n\"updated_by\": \"AI/ML\"\n\"event_status\": \"N\n}, \n\"cdc_id\": 2\n\"table_id\": 2,\n\"column_id\": 236,\n\"event_type\": \"update_table\"\n\"change_type\": \"insert_column\"\n\"last_modified\": \"2025-07-28T11:30:10.837316Z\",\n\"updated_by\": \"AI/ML\",\n\"event_status\": \"N\"\n},{\n\"cdc_id\": 3,\n\"table_id\": 5,\n\"column_id\": 23\n\"event_type\": \"update_table\",\n\"change_type\": \"update_column\",\n\"last_modified\": \"2025-07-28T11:30:10.837316Z\",\n\"updated_by\": \"AI/ML\",\n\"event_status\": \"N\"\n},{\n\"cdc_id\": 4,\n\"table_id\": 1,\n\"column_id\": null,\n\"event_type\": \"update_table\",\n\"change_type\": \"update_table\",\n\"last_modified\": \"2025-07-28T11:30:10.837316Z\",\n\"updated_by\": \"AI/ML\",\n\"event_status\": \"N\"\n},\n\"cdc_id\": 5,\n\"table_id\": 1,\n\"column_id\": null,\n\"event_type\": \"delete_table\",\n\"change_type\": \"delete_table\",\n\"last_modified\": \"2025-07-28T11:30:10.837316Z\",\n\"updated_by\": \"AI/ML\",\n\"event_status\": \"N\"\n}]\nIn total i have three different \"event_type\"\n1. new_table\n2. update_table\n3. delete_table\n    Also i have one or more change_type for different event_type\n    1. for new_table: \"insert_table\"\n    2. for update_table: \"insert_column\", \"update_column\", \"delete_column\", \"update_table\"\n    3. for delete_table: \"delete_table\"\n\nNow i will share the folder structure of the current existing project\nmain.py,\napp.py,\nutils/config.py, helper_functions.py, llm_query.py, load_prompt.py, logger.py, parse_response.py,\nservices/mongodb_processor.py, run_edge_metadata_task.py, run_relationship_inference_task.py, prompts/clean_table_name.txt, mongo_system_prompt.txt, relationship_system_prompt.txt\n\nLETS START WITH STEP BY STEP: FIRST WE WRITE THE CODE ONLY FOR DELETING THE TABLE (event_type: delete_table)\n\n# Delete Table\nStep 1: Find the table which you have received as input and delete it.\nStep 2: Find the tables which are related to it and update them.\nStep 3: Find the  columns which are columns of deleting table and delete them.\n\nLets say i want to delete the table name called Packages i want to delete the everything that particular table is related.\nfor example if the table packages is related with other table you need to update that table, then you need to delete the columns related to that table\n\nYou first give me the structure then we can proceed with one by one file","x":-6168,"y":-8592,"width":488,"height":392}
